00:01:37,270 --> 00:01:45,070
Speaker 1: Over to you, Pastor D. Thank you, Pastor D. So to establish the truth and invalidity of the Bible, there are some questions we just must answer.

22
00:01:45,070 --> 00:01:48,790
Speaker 1: And the first question is the Bible properly copied?

23
00:01:49,110 --> 00:01:50,870
Speaker 1: Is the Bible properly copied?

24
00:01:51,750 --> 00:01:55,190
Speaker 1: And in answering this question, it means we must put the Bible to the same test.

25
00:01:55,600 --> 00:01:58,080
Speaker 1: We put other literatures of history.

26
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:02,640
Speaker 1: And one of such tests is what we call the bibliographical test.

27
00:02:02,640 --> 00:02:04,400
Speaker 1: And it tends to answer the question.

28
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:08,720
Speaker 1: It tries to answer the question, how many manuscripts are available?

29
00:02:10,080 --> 00:02:14,000
Speaker 1: Are its content the same, or at least similar?

30
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:22,180
Speaker 1: And the second question I'll answer those questions is, how short is the interval between the date of the original documents and.

31
00:02:22,250 --> 00:02:26,170
Speaker 1: And the date of the earliest available manuscripts?

32
00:02:26,170 --> 00:02:26,690
Speaker 1: Let me explain.

33
00:02:26,690 --> 00:02:31,930
Speaker 1: A manuscript is simply a book or a document that is handwritten or copied by hand.

34
00:02:32,490 --> 00:02:35,330
Speaker 1: And so the question is, how many manuscripts do we have?

35
00:02:35,330 --> 00:02:38,810
Speaker 1: Let me use an example to just paint the picture before I give us the facts.

36
00:02:39,210 --> 00:02:47,130
Speaker 1: Imagine a lecturer starting a class gives a handout and it says, you know, we're using this material for the next 2000 years.

37
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:50,100
Speaker 1: Of course, the man cannot live 2000 years.

38
00:02:50,100 --> 00:02:59,780
Speaker 1: Now, how do we know after 2000 years that the handout that we still have is the same one that the lecturer gave, knowing that the original copy probably will be missing by that time.

39
00:03:00,260 --> 00:03:06,420
Speaker 1: So the first question is, how many copies of the original handout do we have?

40
00:03:06,820 --> 00:03:09,460
Speaker 1: Now remember, manuscript is handwritten document.

41
00:03:09,700 --> 00:03:20,570
Speaker 1: So if you have only four copies of these handouts, we can question how are we so sure that this is still the same material that the man gave 2,000 years ago?

42
00:03:20,890 --> 00:03:24,170
Speaker 1: Imagine we have 5,000 copies.

43
00:03:24,650 --> 00:03:30,210
Speaker 1: Now to a large extent, if you have 5,000 copies, then we can check those copies and we can come to a good conclusion.

44
00:03:30,210 --> 00:03:31,210
Speaker 1: 5,000 Copies.

45
00:03:31,290 --> 00:03:37,610
Speaker 1: Then to a large extent this could be this could be the same material even though we don't have the original anymore.

46
00:03:37,690 --> 00:03:42,690
Speaker 1: But this can still give us a value, can still help us test the validity of the original document.

47
00:03:42,690 --> 00:03:44,650
Speaker 1: And that's exactly what we're talking about.

48
00:03:44,810 --> 00:04:01,690
Speaker 1: And to give the fact is, in the New Testament alone We have over 5,686 known Greek manuscripts, over 10,000 Latin vulgates and at least 9,300 other versions in the world.

49
00:04:02,330 --> 00:04:06,010
Speaker 1: No other document matches up to this number.

50
00:04:06,090 --> 00:04:13,670
Speaker 1: So what that means is we have this amount of copies of the New Testament alone.

51
00:04:13,670 --> 00:04:21,990
Speaker 1: The Dead Sea scroll was discovered in 1947 in Qumran Cave near the Dead Sea, which is where the scroll got his name from.

52
00:04:22,310 --> 00:04:35,990
Speaker 1: And in the Dead Sea scroll There consisted over 800 scrolls and each contained a portions from the every book of the Old Testament.

53
00:04:36,150 --> 00:04:40,060
Speaker 1: Now the entire manuscript of the Book of Isaiah was found in the Dead Sea scroll.

54
00:04:40,610 --> 00:04:45,090
Speaker 1: And yet the age of the scroll dates back to 75 BC.

55
00:04:45,490 --> 00:05:01,730
Speaker 1: Now comparing the manuscripts of the Book of Isaiah that was found in this scroll with the earliest one we have, which was 1100 years apart, we discovered that it maintains 95% accuracy.

56
00:05:01,810 --> 00:05:04,210
Speaker 1: So go back to the handout example I gave.

57
00:05:04,370 --> 00:05:11,860
Speaker 1: So imagine you compare one that was maybe 1900 years ago and one that is 20 years apart.

58
00:05:12,180 --> 00:05:15,300
Speaker 1: Now up to 1100 years apart.

59
00:05:15,460 --> 00:05:20,820
Speaker 1: Comparing the two copies, we realize that it contains 95% accuracy.

60
00:05:21,060 --> 00:05:28,900
Speaker 1: And the 5% differences are just in spelling and omitted letters and misspelled words.

61
00:05:28,900 --> 00:05:36,300
Speaker 1: Of course, if you aren't copy something, you're bound to make few mistakes, but nothing that changes the core meaning of the word.

62
00:05:36,380 --> 00:05:40,460
Speaker 1: So it means that the Bible passes the bibliographical test.

63
00:05:40,620 --> 00:05:43,100
Speaker 1: And the second question is the years apart.

64
00:05:43,260 --> 00:05:44,700
Speaker 1: The Book of John, for instance.

65
00:05:45,180 --> 00:05:49,540
Speaker 1: The earliest Christian manuscripts available today are fragments of the papyrus.

66
00:05:49,540 --> 00:05:52,460
Speaker 1: Papyrus is a material in which they used in writing back then.

67
00:05:52,460 --> 00:06:01,740
